VST3 support for MusE: findings and implementation plan
=======================================================

Status: steps 1 (headless host), 2 (plugin GUI in Qt5/X11), 3 (scanning and plugin cache, with the
build option from step 4), 5 (effect plugins in MusE) and 6 (instruments) are done. Steps 1 and 2 live
in vst3/, steps 3, 5 and 6 are in MusE itself. Step 7 (polish) is partly done: offline mode, auxiliary
buses, sidechain and the note id / note expression layer. What is left is listed there.


FINDINGS
--------

License
  The VST3 SDK has been MIT licensed since 3.8.0 (October 2025). Earlier versions were
  proprietary or GPLv3. MIT code can be linked into MusE (GPL v2 or later) without
  problems. Keep the SDK copyright and license text when bundling it.

SDK on Linux (verified by building and running it, SDK 3.8.1)
  * CMake based. The SDK repo needs the vstgui4 submodule only when VSTGUI is enabled;
    we turn it off (SMTG_ENABLE_VSTGUI_SUPPORT=OFF) and build no examples.
  * The hosting code is in public.sdk/source/vst/hosting: Module (loads .vst3 bundles and
    reads the factory), PlugProvider (creates component + controller and connects them),
    HostApplication, EventList, ParameterChanges, ProcessData, pluginterfacesupport.
  * The CMake target sdk_hosting does NOT contain module_linux.cpp or plugprovider.cpp,
    the SDK's own host examples add them to their source lists. We do the same.
  * Same for MemoryStream (public.sdk/source/common/memorystream.cpp).
  * The SDK's editorhost example does support Linux (X11 via GTK3/gtkmm, optional
    Wayland preview). The vcpkg manifest that marks hosting examples as unsupported on
    Linux is only a packaging limitation.
  * Module::getModulePaths() already knows ~/.vst3, /usr/lib/vst3, /usr/local/lib/vst3.

Result of the proof of concept, with real plugins (Surge XT, Surge XT Effects, u-he Diva)
  Scanning, instantiation, bus setup, processing, note events, parameters and state
  save/load all work through the SDK hosting classes. Nothing from MusE or Qt is needed
  for that part.

MusE side (existing code that VST3 would plug into)
  * Plugins:      muse/plugin.h  (Plugin, PluginI, PluginIBase, Pipeline)
  * Synths:       muse/synth.h   (Synth, SynthIF, SynthI)
  * VST2 as the closest model: muse/vst_native.{h,cpp}
      VstNativeSynth, VstNativeSynthIF (instrument), VstNativePluginWrapper (effect).
  * Scanning and the plugin cache: libs/plugin/plugin_scan.{h,cpp}, plugin_cache_*.
    Has PluginTypeVST / PluginTypeLinuxVST but no VST3 type.
  * Build: CMakeLists.txt has ENABLE_VST_NATIVE and the bundled vestige header.
  * Threading rule: the GUI must not change song data directly. It goes through
    PendingOperationList (muse/operations.h), see README.developer.undo_system.

Main difficulties, in rough order of effort
  1. Plugin GUIs: IPlugView on X11 needs an IRunLoop (file descriptor and timer handlers)
     from the host and IPlugFrame::resizeView. Embedding into Qt5 is the main Linux risk.
     (Not yet tried, see step 2.)
  2. Parameters: VST3 has no MIDI controllers. MIDI CC, pitch bend and program change
     must be mapped through IMidiMapping (and IUnitInfo for programs). Edits made in the
     plugin GUI arrive through IComponentHandler on the GUI thread and have to reach the
     realtime thread without locks or allocation. The PoC uses a mutex, that must go.
  3. Buses: MusE ports are a flat list, VST3 has buses with speaker arrangements.
     Initially: main bus only, auxiliary buses inactive (what the PoC does).
  4. Events: notes with noteId, note expression, per-note controllers. Start with plain
     note on/off, aftertouch, CC through IMidiMapping.
  5. Threading model: controller on the GUI thread, processor on the realtime thread.
     Plugins that are one object (component also controller) need care.
  6. State: component state + controller state in the song XML (as base64 chunk, like the
     VST2 chunks).
  7. Latency, tail and bypass: map getLatencySamples, getTailSamples and the kIsBypass
     parameter to MusE's PluginLatencyReportingType / PluginBypassType.
  8. Scan safety: plugins crash or hang while loading. The scan should run out of process
     (MusE already has a scan-info cache to keep results).


PLAN
----

Step 1: headless proof of concept                                              DONE
  vst3/vst3host.{h,cpp}  Scan, load, setup, process, notes, params, state.
  vst3/vst3probe.cpp     list / params / render (can write a WAV).
  vst3/tests             15 tests against real installed plugins, run with ctest.
  Exit criteria: load a real instrument and effect and get correct audio. Met.

Step 2: plugin GUI in Qt5 on X11                                               DONE
  vst3/vst3editor.{h,cpp}  Editor, a QWidget hosting IPlugView; QtFrame (IPlugFrame +
                           Linux::IRunLoop on QSocketNotifier / QTimer).
  vst3/vst3gui.cpp         Opens a plugin's editor, keeps it processing, prints GUI edits.
  vst3/tests/vst3_gui_tests.cpp  Needs a display and plugins, skipped (77) without.
  Results with Surge XT, Surge XT Effects and Diva: the editors open at the size the plugin
  asks for, the plugin's X window is mapped inside ours with the same size, open/close
  cycles and destruction order are clean, resizing works (including plugins that keep an
  aspect ratio, which needs the host to resize to what checkSizeConstraint returns), and
  a synthetic mouse drag in the editor produced a parameter edit through IComponentHandler.
  Original plan text:
  Open IPlugView in a QWindow/QWidget (XEmbed or a foreign window), implement IRunLoop
  (QSocketNotifier + QTimer) and IPlugFrame::resizeView, and IComponentHandler edits shown
  in a small Qt test window with the parameter list. Still stand-alone (vst3/).
  Exit criteria: Surge XT and Diva editors open, are usable and close cleanly, repeatedly.
  Then decide on Wayland. Under Wayland run through XWayland first.

Step 3: scanning and the plugin cache in MusE                                  DONE
  What it does: MusE finds *.vst3 bundles in ~/.vst3, /usr/local/lib(64)/vst3 and
  /usr/lib(64)/vst3 (or in $VST3_PATH, colon separated), scans every bundle in the existing
  'muse_plugin_scan' sandbox program, and keeps the result in <scanner dir>/vst3_plugins.scan
  like the other plugin types. Changes (new, removed or modified bundles) are detected as for
  the other types, a bundle that cannot be scanned ends up in unknown_plugins.scan so it is
  not tried again every time. VST3 entries in the plugin list are ignored by the plugin and
  synth dialogs for now.
  Changes:
    libs/plugin/plugin_scan.{h,cpp}   PluginTypeVST3 (0x100), string "VST3", part of PluginTypesAll.
    libs/plugin/plugin_cache_reader   pluginGetVst3Directories(), pluginVst3BundleTime(),
                                      cache file name, existence check and reading.
    libs/plugin/plugin_cache_writer   collecting bundles, scanVst3Plugins() (runs the sandbox
                                      program per bundle), cache creation and dirty check.
    sandbox/vst3_scan.{h,cpp}         writeVst3Info(): uses the vst3/vst3host code to create every
                                      audio class in a bundle, set it up, and write a scan entry.
    sandbox/muse_plugin_scan.cpp      Handles *.vst3 paths when the VST3 type is asked for.
    muse/main.cpp, globals            -3 command line option (don't load VST3), loadVST3.
    muse/*.cpp, synti/                'case PluginTypeVST3' where the plugin types are switched on.
  Mapping of the scan entry: _uri = class id (32 hex), _label/_name = class name, _maker = vendor,
  _description = sub categories ("Instrument|Synth"), class Instrument and/or Effect, audio ports =
  channels of the main buses, control in ports = visible writable parameters (not program change),
  control out ports = read-only parameters, event in port if it takes notes, bypass port if a
  parameter is flagged bypass, flags HasGui (an editor view exists), HasChunks, SupportsTimePosition.
  Control values are normalized 0..1 in the scan, conversion to plain values is for step 5.
  Time stamp of a bundle = newest file in Contents/<arch>/ (the directory's own stamp does not
  change when the library inside is replaced), see setPluginScanFileInfo().
  The first start after enabling VST3 rescans all plugin types, because MusE recreates all cache
  files when one is missing.
  Tests: libs/plugin/tests/vst3_cache_test.cpp (ctest -R vst3_cache) drives the real cache code
  and sandbox program in a temporary directory.

Step 4: build integration                                    PARTLY DONE (needed by step 3)
  Done: ENABLE_VST3 option (default OFF) and VST3_SDK_PATH in CMakeLists.txt, VST3_SUPPORT in
  config.h, the SDK libraries are built from the SDK checkout, muse_vst3host is a static library
  made in sandbox/CMakeLists.txt from vst3/vst3host.cpp. Still to do: a vendored copy of the SDK
  sources, packaging, a default for ENABLE_VST3 and moving vst3host out of vst3/ into muse/.
  Original text: ENABLE_VST3 option and VST3_SDK_PATH (or a vendored copy of the needed SDK sources:
  base, pluginterfaces, public.sdk hosting and common). The code compiles out cleanly
  with VST3_SUPPORT undefined, like LV2 and VST2. Move vst3host into muse/ (or a module).

Step 5: effects                                                                DONE
  VST3 effects can be put in the effects rack of a track, from the plugin dialog (type "VST3").
  muse/vst3.{h,cpp}   Vst3PluginWrapper (a Plugin, in the style of the VST2 and LV2 wrappers, with
                      fake ladspa style ports) and Vst3PluginState (one instance, the 'handle' of a
                      PluginI), initVST3() which makes the wrappers from the plugin cache.
  What works: instantiate, activate (set up for the audio buffer size), process, control inputs
  (values pushed to the plugin when they change, also from automation and the controller graphs),
  read-only parameters as control outputs, bypass through the plugin's bypass parameter, latency
  report, transport info (position, tempo, time signature, playing), state saved in and restored
  from the song file (<customData>vst3-state:base64</customData>, control values follow the restored
  state), the plugin's own editor window (Plugin 'N' button / nativeGuiVisible in the song), and
  edits made in the editor going to the control values, automation recording (begin/edit/end) and
  the processor.
  Threads: apply() runs in the audio thread. It does not lock or allocate: parameter values are
  staged in tables made when the instance was created (Instance::queueParamRT) and values for the
  plugin's edit controller go to the main thread through a lock free ring (controllerUpdateRT,
  applied by the heart beat in applyControllerUpdates). Edits from the plugin go the other way:
  IComponentHandler -> Instance edit callback -> the IPC ring of PluginI -> control value ->
  apply() -> processor. That is why the host side forwarding of edits is turned off.
  Parameter values are normalized 0..1 (like VST2). Value to text conversion is left to the plugin
  (its editor shows it). Stepped parameters are linear 0..1 in MusE. A toggle (step count 1) is a
  check box.
  Port numbering: audio inputs, audio outputs, control inputs, control outputs, the same as in the
  scan. Instance::inputControls() / outputControls() are used by both, so they cannot disagree. If
  the plugin no longer matches the cache the plugin is not instantiated ("rescan the plugins").
  Known limitations:
    * activate() sets the plugin up in the audio thread, as the other plugin types do.
    * Only the main audio buses, no sidechain. No MIDI/note input to effects.
    * The plugin editor needs Qt's xcb platform (X11 or XWayland). With another Qt platform, for
      example native Wayland, the editor button is still there but shows an information dialog that
      says to start MusE with QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb.
    * An edit controller that changes many values at once (kParamValuesChanged) is followed by
      reading all parameters; plugins that never say so are not followed until the next edit.
    * Channel counts: a plugin whose main buses are not what the cache says is passed through.
  Diagnostics, with the -D option: what is instantiated and activated, the first block, the output
  peak, every control value sent to the plugin and every edit seen. MUSE_VST3_TEST_TONE=1 replaces the
  input of the plugin by a 220 Hz sine (for trying without an audio source), and MUSE_VST3_TEST_EDIT=1
  makes the wrapper act as if the plugin reported an edit of its last control.
  How it was tried: a song with the Surge XT Effects plugin on the first output track, run with
  "muse4 -a -D song.med" (Qt xcb): instantiate, activate (512 samples at 44100 Hz), audio flowing
  (output peak 0.238 for a 0.25 input), control values from the song reach the plugin and change the
  sound (peak 0.254), the state made by "vst3probe state" is restored (the controls take the
  values set when it was made), the editor opens inside MusE (600 x 490, same as stand-alone), and
  an edit event reaches the processor through the control value. A real mouse click in the editor
  inside MusE could not be tested here: synthetic X events are not delivered reliably by this
  compositor (see the notes of step 2). The editor and its edit callback are tested stand-alone.
  To make a song file for a test, the plugin goes in the track like this (inside <AudioTrack>):
      <plugin><type>VST3</type><uri>CLASS ID FROM "vst3probe list"</uri><label>NAME</label>
      <name>NAME</name><active>1</active><on>1</on><nativeGuiVisible>1</nativeGuiVisible></plugin>
  Run MusE with APPDIR set to a directory that has usr/local/bin/muse_plugin_scan if the scanner
  that is installed does not match this build (MusE starts the scanner by its installed path).

Step 6: instruments                                                            DONE
  VST3 instruments can be made as synth tracks (synth dialog, type "VST3"), with the plugin's editor,
  programs, state and automation. Effects can still be used as a synth track like the other plugin
  types do. muse/vst3.{h,cpp}: Vst3Synth (a Synth) and Vst3SynthIF (a SynthIF, one instance on a track).
  Host side additions (vst3/vst3host.*), all usable from the audio thread: noteOn/noteOff with sample
  offsets, polyPressure, midiController() (MIDI controllers go to parameters through IMidiMapping,
  pitch bend and channel pressure are controllers 129 and 128, as in the VST3 specification),
  queueParamAtRT() for sample accurate parameter points, programCount/programName/selectProgramRT()
  (the program list of the program change parameter, through IUnitInfo).
  Midi events (MusE's events of the track, from playback and from the user) are cut into slices
  together with the automation controllers like the other synths do, and the events of a slice go to
  the plugin with their frame inside the slice. Notes, controllers, pitch bend, channel and poly
  pressure, program changes (program numbers are numbered in a row: bank high bits are the upper
  bits, so a list with more than 128 programs works) and control changes (to the plugin's own
  parameters if it maps them) are handled.
  The control values of the plugin (automation, the editor, programs that change them) work as for
  effects: the values are in the Port array of the instance, the audio thread tells the plugin what
  changed and the editor follows, edits from the editor come back through the IPC ring.
  Port counts: audio out = channels of the main output bus, audio in = channels of the main input bus
  (instruments with an input are given audio from the routes of the track). Other buses are inactive.
  Diagnostics, with the -D option: activation, output peak and a rough pitch (zero crossings) about
  every two seconds, program selection, state restore. MUSE_VST3_TEST_NOTE=1 plays middle C through
  MusE's own midi event path after a few seconds, selects program 3, bends the note up with a pitch bend
  event and stops it.
  How it was tried, with songs that have a synth track on midi port 1 (see below), "muse4 -a -D":
    Surge XT: peak 0.21 at 261.6 Hz for middle C, 293.4 Hz after the pitch bend (+2 semitones through
    the IMidiMapping), silence after the note off; 'program 3 is not there' (it has no programs);
    state restore (the controls take the values made with vst3probe state); editor opens (1141 x 711)
    Diva: program 3 selected through the program list (the sound changes), latency 16 reported.
  A synth track in a song file (after </AudioTrack> inside <SynthI>):
      <type>VST3</type><uri>CLASS ID</uri><label>NAME</label><name>NAME</name><active>1</active><on>1</on>
      <customData>vst3-state:...</customData><port>0</port><nativeGuiVisible>1</nativeGuiVisible>
  Known limitations:
    * No MIDI output of the plugin (arpeggiators, midi effects), no system exclusive messages, no note
      expression or tuning, and notes get no note id (note offs find their note by channel and pitch).
    * Only the main audio buses, so a multi-output instrument gives only its first bus.
    * More than a handful of parameter changes for one parameter in one slice allocate in the plugin
      host's value queue the first time (the queues are made with room for 5 points).
    * The editor needs Qt's xcb platform, otherwise an information dialog is shown.

Step 7: polish                                                        PARTLY DONE (see the list)
  Done:
  * Offline / freewheel: Instance::setOffline() tells the plugin that the processing is offline
    (ProcessSetup.processMode kOffline). VST3 only allows that at set up time, so a running plugin is
    set up again, from the audio thread like activate() is. Effects and instruments follow
    MusEGlobal::audio->freewheel() at the start of every cycle and do the switch when it changes (exporting
    or bouncing). Playing notes stop at the switch. The scan marks VST3 plugins with a freewheel
    function (PluginFreewheelTypeFunction).
  * Auxiliary output buses (instruments): Instance::SetupOptions::allOutputBuses activates all the audio
    output buses. A synth track now has all the channels as outputs (totalOutChannels) and the main bus
    as its own channels, so the other outputs can be routed from the synth like those of the VST2 and DSSI
    synths. Tried with Surge XT (3 stereo buses) and Six Sines, Seven Outs (7 stereo buses, 14 channels,
    MusE asks for 14 buffers). That plugin makes no sound with only its main bus on, which is one reason
    to use all outputs. Effects (rack plugins) use the main buses only.
  * Sidechain: SetupOptions::sidechainInputs activates the auxiliary input buses. For instruments (and effects
    used on synth tracks) they are extra input channels of the synth track, so they are routed to it like
    other audio inputs. The rack of a track has no sidechain routing in MusE, so effects in the rack
    leave the sidechain bus off, which plugins handle (they use their own signal).
  * Note expression: every note on gets a note id (Instance::noteOn returns it, note off, poly pressure
    and expression use it, a note that is already on is ended first). Instance::noteExpression() sends a
    value for the sounding note, noteExpressionTypes() lists what the plugin understands. Tried with Six
    Sines (Panorama and Tuning): a tuning value for the note raised its pitch. MusE does not send note
    expression values itself, see below.
  Not done:
  * Note expression from MusE: MusE's notes have no per note pitch, pressure or timbre, so there is nothing
    to send. What it takes is MPE (MIDI polyphonic expression): one note per MIDI channel, and the pitch
    bend, pressure and CC 74 of that channel become the tuning, volume/pressure and brightness of that note.
    That needs a setting per synth track (the MPE zone and the bend range) and care for the existing
    channel handling. It is a feature of its own.
  * MIDI output of plugins, native Wayland (for later, with Qt6).
  * Parameter values in real units (they are still normalized), 64 bit processing, sample accurate
    handling of repeated parameter changes in effects, drag and drop of presets.
  Diagnostics, with -D: MUSE_VST3_TEST_FREEWHEEL=1 makes an effect or instrument offline for a while, and
  a line says when the mode changes. The bus layout of an instrument is printed when it is made.

Each step should leave MusE building and releasable. Steps 1 to 2 carry the technical
risk, so they come first.


Notes from step 2
  * Qt5 must use the xcb platform plugin, also on Wayland (runs through XWayland).
    vst3gui and the GUI tests set QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb when it is not set. In MusE the
    application already decides that for the whole process, so a native Wayland MusE
    would not be able to host VST3 editors until the SDK's Wayland support is used.
  * Plugins use the run loop differently: Surge registers 2 file descriptors, Diva a
    timer and a file descriptor. Both are served by the Qt event loop.
  * The plugin's editor must be closed (view removed, frame cleared) before the plugin
    instance is destroyed. Editor::closeView() does that, the Editor destructor too.
  * Screenshots with QScreen::grabWindow are black for XWayland windows, so the tests check
    the X window tree instead (XQueryTree) rather than pixels.
  * The synthetic click test depends on the compositor delivering XTest input. When it is
    not delivered the test only warns (VST3_TEST_REQUIRE_CLICK=1 makes it an error). Moving
    the mouse over the test window while it runs can make it miss.
  * Not done: keyboard focus handling (XEMBED focus), content scale factor, plugins that
    open extra top level windows or popups, drag and drop.


TRYING STEP 5
-------------

  See the end of step 5 above for a test song. Needs a display, Qt xcb, and VST3_PATH if the plugins
  are not in the standard places. Do not run several MusE instances at once when trying the editor.
  vst3probe state "Surge XT Effects" --set 849359077=0.8 --out state.txt   # makes <customData> text

TRYING STEP 3
-------------

  cd build_dir     # out of source, for instance build_vst3
  cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=release -DMODULES_BUILD_STATIC=ON -DENABLE_VST3=ON \
        -DVST3_SDK_PATH=$HOME/dev/vst3sdk ..
  make -j8 muse_plugin_scan vst3_cache_test
  ctest -R vst3_cache --output-on-failure      # needs VST3 plugins in the standard places
  # scan one bundle by hand (-t256 is PluginTypeVST3, the output file must exist):
  touch /tmp/scan.xml
  ./sandbox/muse_plugin_scan -t256 -f "/usr/local/lib/vst3/Surge XT.vst3" -o /tmp/scan.xml -p

TRYING STEP 1
-------------

  cd vst3
  cmake -S . -B build -DVST3_SDK_PATH=/path/to/vst3sdk     # default is ~/dev/vst3sdk
  cmake --build build -j
  ./build/vst3probe list
  ./build/vst3probe params "Surge XT"
  ./build/vst3probe render "Surge XT" --note 60 --seconds 3 --wav /tmp/out.wav
  ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

  Step 2, the plugin editor (needs Qt5 Widgets and a display):
  ./build/vst3gui "Surge XT"                       # opens the editor, hold the button to play C4
  ./build/vst3gui Diva --seconds 10 --wav /tmp/gui.wav

  Get the SDK:  git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/steinbergmedia/vst3sdk
  (the submodule is not needed, since VSTGUI is switched off; plain clone is enough).

  Tests use the plugins found in the standard locations. Environment variables:
    VST3_TEST_DIRS=dir1:dir2        where to look
    VST3_TEST_INSTRUMENT=name       instrument to test with (default: the first found)
    VST3_TEST_EFFECT=name           effect to test with (default: the first found)
  Without any installed plugin the tests report "skipped".
